 The octagon is convex, so each of its interior angles is between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$.  The sum of an octagon's interior angles is $180(8-2) = 1080$ degrees.  So, an interior angle of $180^\circ$ takes away from the sum of 1080 degrees, and an interior angle greater than $180^\circ$ makes the sum less than 1080 degrees.  Hence, the greatest number of angles that can be $90^\circ$ is 7, because $7(90) = 630$, leaving $1080-630 = 450$ degrees for the $(8-7)=1$ interior angle, and $450^\circ < 180^\circ$. However, there is no such polygon with 7 right angles, since if each of 6 interior angles is $90^\circ$, then the sum of the remaining 2 interior angles is $1080 - 6\cdot90 = 180$ degrees, which is not possible because each interior angle of a convex polygon is less than $180$ degrees so it is not even possible to have 6 interior angles of $90$ degrees.
Thus, the maximum number of interior right angles is $\boxed{6}$, since if 6 interior angles have value 90 degrees, there is one 90-degree angle left giving a total of $6\cdot90+90=630$ degrees for the sum of interior angles, which leaves $1080-630=450$ degrees for the remaining interior angle, which is possible since adjacent interior angles sum to $1080^\circ-90^\circ=990^\circ$.

