Human: Let $\mathcal{C}$ be the category of abelian groups, and $\mathcal{D}$ be the category of groups. The inclusion functor $F:\mathcal{D}\to\mathcal{C}$ gives the inclusion of abelian groups in the category of groups. When does this functor become an equivalence?

If $F$ is an equivalence, then $\mathcal{D}$ must have all the same abelian group properties as $\mathcal{C}$. Abelian groups are precisely the commutative groups. If a group is commutative, then it is terminal. This statement is equivalent to the cancellation property of the category, that is, $G\times A \cong G\times B$ implies $A \cong B$. Therefore, it follows that $F$ is an equivalence iff $\mathcal{C}$ is the category of abelian groups.

If $F$ is not an equivalence, then the category $\mathcal{D}$ must have abelian group properties that necessarily come from the category of sets. In other words, $\mathcal{D}$ must consist of certain non-abelian groups as objects such that it still satisfies the inclusion.

