 The cross product of $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$ is \[\begin{pmatrix} (2)(3) - (-6)(1) \\ [-6](1) - (3)(5) \\ (5)(1) - (2)(1) \end{pmatrix} = \boxed{ \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ -21 \\  3\end{pmatrix}}. \]

In general, the cross product of $\begin{pmatrix}a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} k \\ k \\ k \end{pmatrix}$ is \[\begin{pmatrix} (b)(k) - (c)(k) \\ (c)(k) - (a)(k) \\ (a)(k) - (b)(k) \end{pmatrix} = k\begin{pmatrix} b - c \\ c -a \\ a - b \end{pmatrix}.\]This can be proven by noticing that, since $d = a \cdot k + c \cdot k + b \cdot k = 0,$ \[0 = (a \cdot k + c \cdot k + b \cdot k)(1,0,0) = (a)(k) (k,0,0) + (c)(k)(0,k,0) + (b)(k)(0,0,k),\]in other words, \[0 = a(k,0,0) + c(0,k,0) + b(0,0,k).\]Similarly, \[0 = a(k,0,0) + b(0,0,k) + c(0,k,0).\]Thus, the cross product of $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} k \\ k \\ k \end{pmatrix}$ is proportional to any linear combination of $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} k \\ k \\ k \end{pmatrix},$ which are orthogonal and so orthogonal to their cross product, another orthogonal bisector.

Now, $\begin{pmatrix} b - c \\ c -a \\ a - b \end{pmatrix}$ is such a linear combination, orthogonal to $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} k \\ k \\ k \end{pmatrix},$ connecting it to the zero vector, so the direction of the cross product of $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} k \\ k \\ k \end{pmatrix}$ will be in the direction of $\begin{pmatrix} b - c \\ c -a \\ a - b \end{pmatrix}.$

